%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
title: a1.1 Moving in, staying in, greeting and inviting
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Four activities: moving into the care home (SA-01), waiting for a reason to leave the room (SA-02), greeting someone new (SA-03) and inviting someone to spend time together (SA-04). A newcomer arrives confused, with little say, among strangers. Others have stayed in their rooms for months. Shyness, forgotten names and facts, and having nothing to say stop most first contacts. Fewer still talk, held back by fear of a "no" or of intruding. Staff broker most introductions, depending on who is on shift.

= Situated Activity - Moving into the care home [SA-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-01
|Name|Moving into the care home
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Literature and group reasoning only.

* Lai & Arthur (2003): wandering and exit-seeking in people with dementia
* Victor (2012): loneliness in care homes is common
)))
|Goal|Settle in, learn the place and the routine, know some faces and regain some control.
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - moves in
* ST-02: Fellow resident - may welcome
* ST-03: Relative - arranges the move, gives the life story
* ST-04: Care worker - receives and shows around
* ST-05: Activity coordinator - takes the life story, assigns a seat, introduces
* ST-06: Medical and therapy staff - assess walking
)))
|Trigger|Living at home ends after a fall, a hospital stay or a carer who can no longer cope. A place comes free.
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Pack and say goodbye
1. Arrive and unpack
1. Learn the way to the room, toilet, dining room, lounge and garden door
1. Learn the daily routine and house rules
1. Meet staff and residents (SA-03)
1. Give the life story
1. Find a seat at table and a role
)))
|Context|Often unplanned, decided by others, with little say over timing or room. Knows nobody. Staff change by shift, and other residents are often further into dementia and quiet. Doors and corridors look alike, and exits are coded. New surroundings add to confusion. The home sets meals, bedtime and going out.
|Frequency and duration|Once per resident. Settling takes weeks to months.
|Tools and artifacts|Room, name on door, intake form, life-story book, care plan, house rules, activity programme, coded doors
|Existing workarounds|(((
* Own furniture and photos
* A visit before the move
* Life story taken with family
* A named care worker
* Family stay the first day and visit often early on
* Introductions (SA-03)
* Keeping old habits, like the morning paper or a daily walk
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* Does not know why they are here, or thinks it temporary
* Asks to go home and heads for the exit
* Cannot find the room, toilet or dining room
* Grieves for the home, spouse, pet and neighbours left behind
* Resists the routine and care, angry at family or staff
* Nobody to talk to (PS-06)
* Too many new faces to remember
* Seated among residents who do not talk
* Stays in the room, skipping shared meals and activities (SA-02)
* Life story not taken, or not passed on
* Family visits taper
* Not allowed out alone until walking is assessed (ST-06)
* Withdrawal seen late (PS-06)
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01 (supported by ST-02 to ST-06); precedes → SA-02, SA-03; illustrated-by → PS-06 (in part).

= Situated Activity - Waiting for a reason to leave the room [SA-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-02
|Name|Waiting for a reason to leave the room
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Literature and group reasoning only.

* Victor (2012): loneliness in care homes is common
* Clare (2003): people aware of decline protect themselves, or face it
)))
|Goal|A reason worth leaving the room for.
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - waits, then goes out or stays
* ST-03: Relative - visits
* ST-04: Care worker - looks in, brings meals
* ST-05: Activity coordinator - invites to activities
)))
|Trigger|Nothing planned and nobody to see, often a habit built over months
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Get up and dress
1. Pass the time with television, the window or a doze
1. Listen for a knock, a meal or a visit
1. Weigh an invitation
1. Go out, or stay
)))
|Context|Has lived in the home for months or years. The room is the one place they control. Staff look in briefly. Earlier acquaintances have moved, died or been forgotten. The lounge is full of strangers.
|Frequency and duration|Daily, most of the day.
|Tools and artifacts|Room, television, window, call bell, meal tray, activity programme
|Existing workarounds|(((
* Meals in the dining room
* Staff invite to activities, more than once
* A favourite activity, like music or bingo
* A small task, like folding napkins or watering plants
* Family visits and walks
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* No reason comes, and the day passes in the room
* Invitations are to activities, not to see someone
* Declines every invitation, and staff stop asking
* Sleeps by day and lies awake at night
* Mood and walking decline unseen (PS-06)
* Forgets the names of the few people they knew
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01 (supported by ST-03 to ST-05); may follow → SA-01; precedes → SA-03; illustrated-by → PS-06.

= Situated Activity - Greeting someone new [SA-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-03
|Name|Greeting someone new
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Literature and group reasoning only.

* Orange et al. (1996): in Alzheimer's disease, trouble in conversation grows and repair succeeds less often
* Clare (2003): people aware of decline protect themselves, or face it
)))
|Goal|Exchange names and a few words, with a reason to talk again and no face lost.
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - greets, or is greeted
* ST-02: Fellow resident - greets, or is greeted
* ST-03: Relative - introduces
* ST-04: Care worker - introduces
* ST-05: Activity coordinator - assigns seats, introduces
)))
|Trigger|A newcomer (SA-01), a new neighbour at table, a stranger nearby, or a staff introduction
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Notice the other and decide to speak
1. Greet
1. Exchange names
1. Say something about one's town, work or family
1. Ask, listen, answer
1. Find a shared topic
1. Close ("See you at dinner")
1. Next time, recognise them and recall the name
)))
|Context|Staff decide who sits with whom. Memory loss takes names, faces and own facts. Hearing and sight are often poor. Small talk costs effort, and silence feels like failure. The other often has dementia too. Greetings happen in shared rooms, where others watch.
|Frequency and duration|Daily. Seconds to minutes.
|Tools and artifacts|Lounge, dining tables, seating plan, names on doors, name badges, life-story book, photos
|Existing workarounds|(((
* Staff introduce and re-introduce, with names and one shared fact
* Staff introduce residents who share a town, trade or church
* Seat a newcomer beside a talkative resident
* Names on doors or badges
* Safe topics like the weather, food or the view
* Habitual greetings ("Good morning", a handshake)
* Life-story book or photos as a prompt
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* Too shy to speak first
* Greets a known face as new, or the reverse
* Forgets the name and asks again, embarrassed
* Forgets own facts and goes quiet
* Runs out of things to say, and one leaves
* Repeats a question, and the other notices
* Mishears and nods along
* Offends by being too familiar or too formal
* A greeting goes unheard and is read as a snub
* Talk stops once staff leave
* Ignored when joining a group mid-talk
* Residents with a shared past never find out
* Staff share a personal fact without consent (PS-05)
* Introductions follow who is on shift, not residents' wishes (PS-03)
* Avoids the other after an awkward meeting
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01, ST-02 (supported by ST-03 to ST-05); preceded-by → SA-01, SA-02; precedes → SA-04; illustrated-by → PS-03, PS-05 (in part).

= Situated Activity - Inviting someone to spend time together [SA-04] =

**Report Format:** §1.1 Operational Demands

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|SA-04
|Name|Inviting someone to spend time together
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Provisional. Literature and group reasoning only.

* de Medeiros et al. (2012): residents with dementia form and keep friendships
* Victor (2012): loneliness in care homes is common
* Woods et al. (2018): reminiscence slightly improves communication, mood and quality of life
* Astell et al. (2010): a touchscreen of old photos, music and film supports conversation
* Särkämö et al. (2014): singing and listening to familiar songs improve mood and memory
* Gonzalez & Kirkevold (2014): garden and horticultural activities increase social engagement and reduce agitation
* Camp & Skrajner (2004): residents with milder dementia can lead activities for others
* Spector et al. (2003): themed group activities improve cognition and quality of life
)))
|Goal|Spend time with another resident over something both enjoy. Either can say no without losing face.
|Performing Actor(s)|(((
* ST-01: Resident - invites
* ST-02: Fellow resident - accepts or declines
* ST-03: Relative - includes another resident in a visit
* ST-04: Care worker - suggests an activity, sets it up, opens the garden door
* ST-05: Activity coordinator - seats and groups residents, provides materials
)))
|Trigger|Loneliness, someone sitting alone, fine weather, a shared interest, a staff suggestion, or a good first meeting (SA-03)
|Constituent Tasks|(((
1. Pick someone
1. Find an opening
1. Read interest
1. Suggest something to do, and a time
1. Accept or decline
1. Remember the time, meet and begin
1. Find a way to finish, and perhaps to meet again
)))
|Context|(((
Invitations happen in shared rooms. Others hear, so a "no" is public and a "yes" hard to refuse. A shared task gives the two something to talk about and makes pauses comfortable. Many activities need staff to set them up, unlock the garden or kitchen, or go along; walking is assessed first (ST-06).

The following activities suit two people. Those with a reference were studied with people with dementia, often in groups:

* A card or board game, a jigsaw, a crossword, the newspaper, baking, laying tables
* A walk in the garden, often suggested by staff for the exercise and fresh air
* Potting, watering or picking in a raised bed (Gonzalez & Kirkevold, 2014)
* Looking through photos, music or objects from the past (Woods et al., 2018), or a touchscreen collection of them (Astell et al., 2010)
* Singing or listening to familiar songs (Särkämö et al., 2014)
* Word games, quizzes and sorting objects into categories (Spector et al., 2003)
* Montessori-based games, such as memory bingo, which a resident with milder dementia can lead for another (Camp & Skrajner, 2004)
)))
|Frequency and duration|Rare. Minutes to an hour.
|Tools and artifacts|Lounge, dining tables, seating plan, activity programme, staff knowledge, garden door, raised beds, cards and board games, jigsaws, photo albums, old objects, song books, music player, touchscreen, newspapers
|Existing workarounds|(((
* Staff suggest an activity for two, often a walk
* Residents meet again and again at the same table or activity
* A group activity serves as an opening
* A relative includes a second resident in a visit
* A resident joins someone already busy, in the garden or at a jigsaw
* Staff give two residents a shared task, like watering plants or laying tables
)))
|Observed breakdowns|(((
* The greeting failed, or never happened (SA-03)
* Does not ask, for fear of a "no" or of being a bother
* Hearing loss or a flat expression is read as "no"
* Agrees to please when asked in front of others (PS-04)
* Neither knows how to finish, and the activity runs on too long (PS-04)
* The activity is too hard for one of them, who feels exposed
* An invitation is read as courtship, and the inviter avoided
* Both forget the agreement
* No staff are free to set up, unlock or go along
* In a group of three, one is left out
* Never asks again after one "no"
)))

== Graph edges ==

performed-by → ST-01, ST-02 (supported by ST-03 to ST-05); preceded-by → SA-03; illustrated-by → PS-04.

